---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/utils';
import Animations from '../../components/Animations.vue';
import CommentsSection from '../../components/CommentsSection.vue';
import BlogNavigation from '../../components/BlogNavigation.astro';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { lang: 'en' } },
    { params: { lang: 'es' } },
    { params: { lang: 'pt' } },
  ];
}

const { lang } = Astro.params;
const t = useTranslations(lang as any);

const content = {
  en: {
    title: "Dear Diary, Today's Massive PHP Security Release (18 CVEs & 7 Critical 9.2 Bugs): Why You Need to Patch Right Now 🌸",
    date: "November 20, 2026",
    subtitle: "Breaking down PHP 8.2.36, 8.3.37, 8.4.28 & 8.5.13: PHP-FPM Request Smuggling, Session TOCTOU, Use-After-Free & PHP2Shell fixes by Jill",
    back: 'Back to Home',
    intro: "If you run WordPress, Laravel, Symfony, or custom PHP microservices in production, drop your coffee and check your server versions right now. On <strong>November 19, 2026</strong>, The PHP Foundation dropped a massive emergency security release fixing <strong>18 vulnerabilities</strong>—with <strong>7 rated Critical (CVSS 9.2)</strong>.",
    introDesc: "From single-request Remote Code Execution via PHP-FPM Request Smuggling (CVE-2026-129289) to Session TOCTOU race conditions, Use-After-Free memory bugs in epoll, and the finalized patch for CVE-2026-115560, here is the complete engineering breakdown of what broke and how to protect your servers.",
    bannerLabel: "Official PHP Security Advisories & Release Changelog:",
    bannerUrl: "https://www.php.net/ChangeLog-8.php",
    sections: [
      {
        heading: "1. The Patch Matrix: Which PHP Versions Are Safe?",
        paragraphs: [
          "Every active PHP branch received urgent security updates. If your container image or VPS is running anything older than the numbers below, your stack is exposed:",
          "• **PHP 8.2** → Upgrade immediately to **8.2.36**",
          "• **PHP 8.3** → Upgrade immediately to **8.3.37**",
          "• **PHP 8.4** → Upgrade immediately to **8.4.28**",
          "• **PHP 8.5** → Upgrade immediately to **8.5.13**",
          "Remember: simply running `apt upgrade` or rebuilding your Docker image isn't enough if you forget to **restart the `php-fpm` worker pool** or web server processes so the patched binaries load into memory."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "2. CVE-2026-129289 (CVSS 9.2): PHP-FPM Request Smuggling",
        paragraphs: [
          "The headliner of this release is **CVE-2026-129289**, an HTTP request smuggling bug inside **PHP-FPM** that allows an unauthenticated attacker to achieve **Remote Code Execution (RCE)** with worker privileges using a single crafted request.",
          "How does it happen? Modern reverse proxies like **Nginx, Apache, HAProxy, and LiteSpeed (LSAPI)** keep persistent FastCGI connections open to PHP-FPM workers to avoid TCP handshake overhead.",
          "When an application opens `php://input` to inspect a request body—for instance, a webhook signature verifier or custom router—and **returns early without draining the entire request stream**, the leftover bytes remain sitting in the shared FastCGI socket buffer. The next FastCGI framing header gets desynchronized, letting an attacker smuggle arbitrary FastCGI parameters (like `PHP_VALUE` or `auto_prepend_file`) directly into PHP-FPM."
        ],
        codeLabel: "How Unread php://input Streams Enable FastCGI Request Smuggling",
        code: `[ ATTACKER HTTP REQUEST ]
  POST /webhook.php HTTP/1.1
  Content-Length: 450
  [ Body: Partial payload + Smuggled FastCGI Parameters ]
         │
         ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ REVERSE PROXY (Nginx / Apache / HAProxy)                             │
│ • Forwards request over a REUSED persistent FastCGI socket           │
└───────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┘
                                │
                                ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ UNPATCHED PHP-FPM WORKER (CVE-2026-129289)                           │
│ 1. App opens php://input (e.g., reads first 64 bytes for header check)│
│ 2. App exits early without reading remaining bytes                   │
│ 3. Leftover bytes stay in socket buffer and corrupt the next frame   │
│ 4. Attacker injects PHP_VALUE / auto_prepend_file ──► RCE!           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘`
      },
      {
        heading: "3. Session Attacks & The Finalized PHP2Shell Fix (CVE-2026-115560 & CVE-2026-126714)",
        paragraphs: [
          "Two other CVSS 9.2 critical bugs target PHP's session subsystem:",
          "• **CVE-2026-115560 (Session Serializer / Upload Progress Flaw):** Remember the October 'PHP2Shell' warnings we talked about earlier? This release delivers the complete, finalized upstream fix for the serializer mismatch and input validation bug tied to upload progress and shared session storage.",
          "• **CVE-2026-126714 (Session TOCTOU Race Condition):** A Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use race condition in session file write paths. If an attacker wins the race during concurrent session writes and the target app includes popular Composer gadget chains (like **Guzzle** or **Monolog**), it can trigger arbitrary deserialization and code execution."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "4. Memory Corruption, Local Privilege Escalation & PHAR Bypass",
        paragraphs: [
          "Beyond request smuggling and sessions, the release cleans up several dangerous C-level memory bugs and cryptographic flaws:",
          "• **Engine Use-After-Free (CVE-2026-129182 & CVE-2026-129474):** Use-After-Free vulnerabilities in core engine internals and magic method backtraces that cause memory corruption and potential RCE.",
          "• **PHP-FPM `epoll` Use-After-Free (CVE-2026-129291):** A memory flaw in PHP-FPM's Linux `epoll` event loop that can be abused for **Local Privilege Escalation (LPE)**—meaning a compromised low-privilege worker could escalate on the host.",
          "• **Timezone Cache UAF (CVE-2026-129496):** A Use-After-Free inside PHP's date/timezone caching layer.",
          "• **PHAR Signature Downgrade (CVE-2026-128090):** An OpenSSL signature bypass in PHAR archives caused by an algorithm downgrade attack, allowing tampered `.phar` packages to pass integrity checks."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "5. Emergency Checklist for Sysadmins & DevOps",
        paragraphs: [
          "Don't wait for automated vulnerability scanners to ping your Slack channel at 3 AM. Run through this checklist today:",
